<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ai-Khanoum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300–145 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ai-Khanoum is the site of a Hellenistic city at the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> and Kokcha rivers in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, probably founded in the early Seleucid period around 300 BCE. Excavated by French archaeologists from 1964 to 1978, it revealed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a>, palace and Greek inscriptions, making it the best-known city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom</a>. It was abandoned around 145 BCE after nomad invasions, and the site was heavily looted during the Afghan wars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum.jpg" alt="Ai-Khanoum" width="320" height="185" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Artacoana · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AiKhanumCapitals.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ai-Khanoum, meaning Lady Moon, lies in Takhar Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a>, or Oxus, and the Kokcha, in a triangular plain of about 300 square kilometres bounded by the two rivers and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>. The loess soil suited farming, irrigation canals could be built from the rivers, and the highlands gave herders summer pasture. Mines on the upper Kokcha were the world's only source of lapis lazuli, and also yielded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, iron, lead and rubies.</p><p>The city, whose original name is unknown, was divided between a lower town and a 60-metre acropolis and surrounded by fortifications that were maintained and improved. It controlled access to mining in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> and to strategic choke points, though it did not stand on a major trade route.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city was probably founded between 300 and 285 BCE by an official acting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a> or his son Antiochus I Soter. It was once thought to have been a foundation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, perhaps as Alexandria Oxiana, but this is now considered unlikely. About 1.5 kilometres to the north the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> had built a fort called Kohna Qala. The Greco-Bactrian secession under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodotus-i">Diodotus I</a>, around 250 BCE, halted Seleucid building, and the city probably became mainly military. It grew again under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euthydemus-i">Euthydemus I</a> and Demetrius I, and most of the ruins date from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucratides-i">Eucratides I</a>, who redeveloped it and may have renamed it Eucratideia.</p><p>Soon after his death about 145 BCE the Greco-Bactrian kingdom collapsed, and Saka invaders captured the city, which was generally abandoned, though parts were occupied now and then until the 2nd century CE. The arsenal held few weapons and its passages were blocked with brick, which suggests that the main army was in the field when the city fell.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In 1838 the British explorer John Wood was told of an ancient city there, but local people would tell him nothing. In 1961 King Mohammed Zahir Shah, on a hunting trip, was shown a Corinthian column capital found at the site and called in the French Archaeological Delegation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Daniel Schlumberger and then Paul Bernard led the dig. They found a palace of about 350 by 250 metres, a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> for 6,000 spectators, an arsenal, two sanctuaries, Greek inscriptions, coins and ceramics. The ruins lay close to the surface because the city was never resettled. The Soviet-Afghan War halted the work in the late 1970s, and the site was extensively looted in the conflicts that followed.</p><p>The finds mattered because scholars such as Alfred Foucher had searched in vain for physical proof of Hellenistic culture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and Foucher had dismissed it as the Greco-Bactrian mirage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ai-Khanoum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Only a few of its cities have been excavated, among them <b>Ai-Khanoum</b> and Bactra (modern Balkh).</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Other Saka groups pushed into Bactria, where nomadic invaders, whether Saka or Yuezhi is debated, destroyed the Greek city at <b>Ai-Khanoum</b> around 145 BCE, and into eastern Iran.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…between 334 and 323 BCE, transformed Greek into a world language, and under the Hellenistic kingdoms that followed it was the language of government, trade and culture from Egypt to Afghanistan, where a Greek inscription of maxims copied from Delphi was set up at <b>Ai-Khanoum</b> on the Oxus.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>It has also been suggested that the Delphic maxims inscribed at <b>Ai-Khanoum</b> around 300 BCE, which resemble the edicts in wording and content, may have influenced them.</blockquote><a href="/work/edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>At <b>Ai Khanoum</b>, on the Oxus in what is now northern Afghanistan, excavators found a Greek city with a gymnasium and a theatre, and an inscription recording that a man named Clearchus had copied maxims from the shrine of Apollo at Delphi and set them up there, some…</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
